abstract:

This paper explores the legal, ethical, and institutional necessity of implementing open disclosure legislation in Bangladesh’s healthcare system, where the absence of formal obligations to disclose medical errors undermines transparency, patient rights, and professional accountability. Using a doctrinal legal research methodology, the paper critically analyzes existing Bangladeshi laws, professional codes, and policy documents to reveal their failure to mandate disclosure or protect practitioners who report in good faith. It also explores systemic barriers – including cultural norms, fear of liability, and institutional hierarchies – that obstruct open communication following adverse medical events. The paper argues for a context-sensitive legislative reform, either through a standalone Open Disclosure Act or by amending current frameworks. It proposes a multi-tiered reform agenda including legal codification, institutional restructuring, clinician training, and patient education. Drawing on public accountability theory, medical ethics, and international human rights law (notably Article 12 of the
ICESCR
), the paper positions open disclosure as a developmental, moral, and legal imperative. It contributes to interdisciplinary scholarship by linking legal reform to health system responsiveness, transparency, and patient empowerment – offering a model relevant to other low- and middle-income countries seeking to improve institutional accountability and healthcare governa
